// ============================================================================
//  BUSINESS ERP — PROJECT BACKLOG  (structured TODO checklist)
//  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  WORKFLOW RULES (from the ERP spec):
//   • Implement ONLY the first uncompleted [ ] task below, in order.
//   • After implementing a task: write its validation tests, then flip its
//     [ ] to [x], then STOP and await review before starting the next task.
//   • Do NOT begin implementation work until the backlog is declared complete
//     and the go-ahead is given.
//  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  GENERATOR: business-erp ("Business ERP") — based on the business-template
//  generator (config below + src/template.css + src/template.js). Pipeline
//  partners this ERP reads from / writes to: idea-incubator (idea bundles →
//  qualified leads), project-master (project seeds ⇄ state), the-ledger
//  (ledger reconciliation). Master-data core shared by all modules: parties,
//  catalog, chart of accounts.
//
//  PHASE 1 — ERP CORE: SHELL & DOCUMENT STORE
//  [x] 1. Module shell & navigation — app frame with header + side navigation
//         listing every module (Dashboard, CRM, Sales, Purchasing, Inventory,
//         Projects, Finance, Reports), role-aware module visibility,
//         consistent empty/loading/error states, layouts that work at phone
//         and desktop widths.
//  [x] 2. Canonical document store — persist every module's data as versioned
//         JSON documents under the ERP's own storage namespace (one document
//         per module, split by fiscal year where documents grow large), with
//         locally cached edit keys and a fast local cache: records survive
//         across devices/reloads and no write exceeds the storage ceiling.
//  [x] 3. Sync, conflict & concurrency handling — reconcile the local cache
//         against its canonical document on every startup; when the same
//         document was edited on two devices since last sync, offer
//         keep-mine / keep-theirs / field-level-merge resolution before any
//         overwrite, and never silently discard either side's changes.
//  [x] 4. Backup & restore — one-click full backup (downloadable file PLUS a
//         published backup document covering every module) and a validated
//         restore-from-backup flow; all writes idempotent so re-publishing
//         identical content is a safe no-op.
//  [x] 5. Capacity & archival — capacity view showing each module document's
//         size against the storage ceiling, plus guided archival of old-period
//         documents (e.g. closed prior fiscal years) to a clearly labeled
//         read-only archive that remains retrievable on demand.
//
//  PHASE 2 — MASTER DATA & CONFIGURATION
//  [x] 6. Party directory — one master list of parties typed customer /
//         supplier / both, each with contacts, addresses, tax id, payment
//         terms, credit limit, active status; a single rename/merge updates
//         the whole system.
//  [x] 7. Product & service catalog — entries typed product or service with
//         unit of measure, default sale price, default cost, tax treatment,
//         active flag; used consistently by sales, purchasing, inventory and
//         invoicing.
//  [x] 8. Chart of accounts & tax setup — configurable chart (codes, names,
//         account classes) plus the default posting accounts the system
//         auto-uses (AR, AP, inventory, COGS, sales revenue, tax collected,
//         tax paid) and the tax-rate definitions applied to document lines.
//  [x] 9. Fiscal settings & document numbering — business profile (name,
//         address, currency, fiscal-year start) and sequential per-type
//         document numbering (prefixes, zero-padded counters) that never
//         reuses a number even after deletion, enforced across every module.
//
//  PHASE 3 — CRM & OPPORTUNITY PIPELINE
//  [x] 10. CRM records — per-party record with source, owner, status,
//          chronological activity/notes timeline, and follow-up reminders
//          whose overdue items surface on the dashboard.
//  [x] 11. Opportunity pipeline — stages (lead → qualified → proposal →
//          won/lost), expected value, close date, probability; stage changes
//          and wins are timestamped actions; a won opportunity can create a
//          sales order or project in one step.
//  [x] 12. CRM views — pipeline board, list, and per-party history views with
//          totals and expected revenue by stage, filterable by owner, stage,
//          date range.
//  [x] 13. Pipeline ports — import idea-incubator bundles so incubated ideas
//          arrive as qualified leads; export won opportunities as project
//          seeds consumable by project-master.
//
//  PHASE 4 — SALES: QUOTES, ORDERS, INVOICES
//  [x] 14. Quotes — catalog line items, manual line editing, discounting,
//          automatic tax, validity/expiry, draft/sent/accepted/declined
//          states; accepting converts to a sales order without retyping.
//  [x] 15. Sales orders — created from accepted quotes or entered directly,
//          per-line fulfillment state, delivery promises, status transitions
//          (open → confirmed → shipped → delivered → invoiced), each stamped.
//  [x] 16. Invoicing from orders — invoices generated from delivered order
//          lines with partial invoicing allowed; each carries order + party
//          references and posts automatically to the ledger when created.
//  [x] 17. Backorders & stock checks — on confirmation check lines against
//          available stock, split shippable vs backordered, raise purchasing
//          shortfall automatically, relieve backorders in order-date order as
//          stock arrives.
//  [x] 18. Credit notes — full or partial credit notes against invoices with a
//          required reason; reverse the invoice's ledger postings and mark
//          the invoice credited.
//
//  PHASE 5 — PURCHASING & INVENTORY
//  [x] 19. Inventory & stock movements — stock per product/location from an
//          append-only movement log (receipt, issue, adjustment, transfer)
//          with dates; current quantities derived, never free-edited.
//  [x] 20. Purchase orders — lines, expected dates, receipt status; goods
//          receipt posts stock-in at purchase cost and raises the supplier
//          bill for the ledger in the same action.
//  [x] 21. Bills & supplier payments — bills from POs or direct, due dates and
//          aging; payments post cash-out and clear the referenced bill.
//  [x] 22. Reorder suggestions — reorder points from current stock and recent
//          usage, presented as a suggested PO that can be finalized and sent
//          in one action.
//  [x] 23. Stock valuation & COGS — moving-average valuation derived from the
//          movement log; each sale posts COGS automatically so inventory
//          value ties out to the ledger at any date.
//
//  PHASE 6 — PROJECTS, TIME & SERVICE DELIVERY
//  [x] 24. Projects — linked to a party and optionally a sales order, with
//          milestones (statuses, planned dates) and whole-project
//          profitability vs budget.
//  [x] 25. Tasks & timesheets — tasks under projects/milestones with assignees
//          and states; time entries (date, hours, billable flag, rate);
//          running billable-time vs budget totals on the project.
//  [x] 26. Expenses & progress billing — record project expenses (linked to
//          purchases or manual) and generate progress invoices from completed
//          milestones or logged billable time through the same invoice
//          mechanics as sales.
//  [x] 27. Project state ports — publish project/billing-event bundles for the
//          earlier pipeline; ingest project-master's and the-ledger's
//          receipts back into the ERP project timeline.
//
//  PHASE 7 — FINANCE: LEDGER, AR/AP, PERIOD CLOSE
//  [x] 28. Double-entry journals — balanced debit/credit lines with source
//          document reference and memo; unbalanced entries rejected; posted
//          entries immutable except via reversing entry (full audit trail).
//  [x] 29. Automatic postings map — every money/stock event posts to the
//          configured accounts at correct dates (invoice → AR+revenue+tax;
//          payment → bank+AR; bill → expense/inventory+AP; goods sale →
//          COGS+inventory); any posting failure stops the action and names
//          the offending document.
//  [x] 30. Receivables & payables — per-party balances with aging buckets
//          (current/30/60/90+), statement view, payment application with
//          partial payments and splits across invoices.
//  [x] 31. Bank & cash accounts — manual register plus statement-matching
//          workflow marking transactions cleared so reported cash reconciles
//          to the bank statement.
//  [x] 32. Period close & financial statements — trial balance with drill-down
//          to every posted entry, P&L and balance sheet for any date range,
//          fiscal-period close that locks posting periods and rolls net
//          income to retained earnings, with a documented reopen procedure.
//
//  PHASE 8 — DASHBOARD, REPORTS & DATA UTILITIES
//  [x] 33. Dashboard KPIs — cash position, receivables/payables, open orders
//          and POs, overdue invoices/bills/follow-ups, revenue this period vs
//          last, top customers, low-stock alerts; each KPI clickable through
//          to its source list.
//  [x] 34. Report library — saved report definitions per module (sales by
//          period/customer/product, purchasing spend, project profitability,
//          aged receivables, inventory valuation, tax summary); every report
//          exportable to CSV and printable.
//  [x] 35. Guided CSV import — parties, catalog items, opening balances, stock
//          counts from CSV with a preview-and-fix step reporting each
//          row-level error before anything is committed.
//  [x] 36. Audit log — read-only, searchable, chronological log of every
//          state-changing action (created/edited, status changed, posted or
//          reversed, settings changed) with actor, timestamp, before/after
//          summary.
//
//  PHASE 9 — QUALITY, TESTS & DOCUMENTATION
//  [x] 37. Accounting invariant checks — books always balance (every posted
//          journal sums to zero; AR/AP tie to invoice/bill states; inventory
//          value ties to the movement log; cash ties to the register), re-run
//          after every finance-affecting action, shown as a visible
//          system-health indicator.
//  [x] 38. Round-trip fixture suite — fixture documents for every module plus
//          earlier-pipeline bundles (incl. older schema versions and
//          malformed payloads), with automated tests for store save/load, sync
//          conflicts, backup/restore, and full quote → order → delivery →
//          invoice → payment chains.
//  [x] 39. Error copy & recovery — every failure mode (document too large,
//          quota reached, lost edit key, offline divergence, schema mismatch,
//          unbalanced entry) shows a plain-language, actionable message with
//          the exact next step.
//  [x] 40. README & module playbook — architecture (module framework, document
//          store layout, numbering rules, automatic-posting map, backup
//          schedule), full user workflow per module, and the checklist for
//          adding a new ERP module (declare documents → numbering →
//          auto-postings → register in navigation → dashboard KPIs).
//
//  PHASE 10 — OPTIONAL: MULTI-USER & REALTIME COLLABORATION
//  [ ] 41. Role & access model — roles (owner/manager/staff) scoped per
//          module; every action authorized server-side, not just hidden in
//          the UI.
//  [ ] 42. Realtime hub — companion hub on realtime server capabilities keeps
//          a lightweight index of document versions and broadcasts change
//          events per module topic while payloads stay in the document store.
//  [ ] 43. Concurrent editing experience — two users on the same record see
//          each other's saved changes live with a changed marker and inline
//          conflict resolution, degrading gracefully to polling when the hub
//          is unreachable.
//  [ ] 44. Multi-user audit & rate control — hub auth by role, rate-limited
//          connections grouped by coarse network signals; every remote change
//          flows through the same idempotent version-checked document layer
//          so the audit log records the true actor even in multi-user mode.
// ============================================================================
$meta
  title = Business ERP
  description = A small-business ERP for Perchance — CRM & opportunities, sales (quotes → orders → invoices), purchasing & inventory, projects & time, and double-entry bookkeeping on one shared master-data core, built on the business-template foundation.
  image = https://user.uploads.dev/file/2ae7aade766d2ceb4effdd9f079186b5.jpg
  tags = erp, business, crm, sales, purchasing, inventory, projects, accounting
  header 
    mode = minimal

superFetch = {import:super-fetch-plugin}
charts = {import:data-visualization-plugin}
uploadPlugin = {import:upload-plugin}
generateText = {import:ai-text-plugin}
createServerSocket = {import:server-plugin}

// ============================================================================
//  BUSINESS TEMPLATE  —  CONFIGURATION
// ============================================================================
//
//  Everything the page shows lives in the `config` list below — this is the
//  place to fill in YOUR company and product details. The page renders
//  entirely from it, and the settings panel (gear button, bottom-right)
//  can also be used to preview colors, scan a website for brand-matching
//  color schemes, and import/export the whole configuration as JSON.
//
//  ── JSON import/export format ─────────────────────────────────────────────
//  The exported JSON mirrors this structure exactly. Imports may be PARTIAL —
//  any missing field falls back to these defaults. The full field-by-field
//  guide with types lives in the settings panel → "Import & Export".
//
//  {
//    schemaVersion: "1.0.0",
//    branding:  { companyName, companyShortName, companyUrl, logoGlyph, logoUrl,
//                 tagline, contactEmail, contactPhone, address, footerText },
//    theme:     { mode: "light"|"dark", accentStyle: "gradient"|"solid", radius,
//                 fontSize (15–18), fontFamily, reduceMotion (true|false) },
//    colors:    { primary, secondary, accent, background, surface,
//                 text, textMuted },                     // hex colors
//    product:   { name, badge, headline, headlineAccent, subheadline,
//                 primaryCta, primaryCtaUrl, secondaryCta, secondaryCtaUrl,
//                 status },
//    trustedBy: [ "Client name", ... ],
//    features:  [ { icon, title, description }, ... ],
//    stats:     [ { value, suffix, label }, ... ],
//    how:       [ { title, description }, ... ],
//    showcase:  { heading, description, points: [ ... ] },
//    testimonials: [ { quote, name, role, company }, ... ],
//    cta:       { heading, description, buttonText, buttonUrl },
//    manual:    { heading, description, chapters: [ { title, body }, ... ] },
//    faq:       [ { question, answer }, ... ],
//    footer:    { columns: [ { heading, links: [ { label, url }, ... ] }, ... ] },
//    admin:     { showSettingsButton }
//  }
//
//  Editing tips:
//  • Add or remove list items in any `…` list (features, stats, faq …) —
//    just add/remove numbered items like f1, f2, …
//  • Strings are written raw (no quotes). Numbers and true/false are kept
//    as numbers/booleans automatically.
//  • In footerText, \{year\} and \{companyName\} are placeholders that get
//    filled in automatically.
// ============================================================================

config
  schemaVersion = 1.0.0

  // ── Company / brand identity ─────────────────────────────────────────────
  branding
    companyName = Business ERP         // ← your company name (header, footer, copyright)
    companyShortName = Business ERP    // compact variant for narrow screens (optional)
    companyUrl = https://perchance.org/business-erp  // ← your website (links the logo & footer)
    logoGlyph = B                      // single letter/monogram in the logo mark
    logoUrl =                          // optional square logo image — upload it in the
                                       //   settings panel (Branding → Logo image) to get a
                                       //   hosted URL; shown instead of the letter mark
    tagline = CRM, sales, stock, projects and books — one system that stays in sync
    contactEmail = hello@your-company.com
    contactPhone = +1 (555) 010-0000
    address = 123 Main Street, Suite 100, Anytown, USA
    footerText = © \{year\} \{companyName\}. All rights reserved.

  // ── Visual theme ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  theme
    mode = light                      // light | dark
    accentStyle = gradient            // gradient | solid (how CTAs & highlights are filled)
    radius = 16                       // corner radius in px
    fontSize = 16                     // base text size in px (15 = small, 16 = normal, 18 = large)
    fontFamily = lato                 // font pairing: lato | inter | roboto | open-sans | source-sans |
                                      //   ibm-plex | dm-sans | manrope | montserrat-lato |
                                      //   playfair-lato | merriweather-inter
    reduceMotion = false              // true disables animations & transitions

  // ── Brand palette (hex colors) ───────────────────────────────────────────
  colors
    primary = #0a58ca                 // trust blue — main brand color, buttons, links, highlights
    secondary = #6ea8fe               // soft blue — gradients, charts, avatars
    accent = #084298                  // deep blue — stars, badges, small details
    background = #f8f9fa              // page background
    surface = #ffffff                 // cards & section backgrounds
    text = #212529                    // main text
    textMuted = #6c757d               // secondary text

  // ── Product / project — the hero section ──────────────────────────────────
  product
    name = Business ERP
    badge = Small-business ERP
    headline = Run your whole business on one system.
    headlineAccent = one system.      // word(s) highlighted in the brand gradient
    subheadline = Quotes become orders, orders become invoices, stock moves, projects track time, and every transaction flows into the books — automatically.
    primaryCta = Get started
    primaryCtaUrl = #
    secondaryCta = See how it works
    secondaryCtaUrl = #how
    status = ERP core · rolling out     // small status chip under the CTAs (empty to hide)

  // ── "Trusted by" strip ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  trustedBy = [["Acme Corp","Globex Industries","Initech","Umbrella Systems","Stark Enterprises","Wayne Group"]]

  // ── Feature grid (3–8 cards) ──────────────────────────────────────────────
  features
    f1
      icon = zap
      title = Instant deployments
      description = Ship updates in seconds with automated build and release pipelines baked in from day one.
    f2
      icon = shield
      title = Enterprise-grade security
      description = Role-based access, audit logs, and encryption at rest keep your data protected at every layer.
    f3
      icon = globe
      title = Global by default
      description = Run in regions worldwide with automatic failover and a 99.99% uptime guarantee.
    f4
      icon = chart
      title = Actionable analytics
      description = Real-time dashboards and reports give your team the visibility it needs to make confident decisions.
    f5
      icon = layers
      title = Modular architecture
      description = Clean, documented APIs and pluggable components make the system easy to extend and maintain.
    f6
      icon = code
      title = Developer friendly
      description = Comprehensive docs, typed SDKs, and a CLI that fits naturally into your existing workflow.

  // ── Stats band ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  stats
    s1
      value = 99.99
      suffix = %
      label = Uptime SLA
    s2
      value = 12
      suffix = k+
      label = Active users
    s3
      value = 40
      suffix = +
      label = Countries served
    s4
      value = 24
      suffix = /7
      label = Dedicated support

  // ── Analytics charts (business graphs) ───────────────────────────────────
  // Rendered with the data-visualization-plugin. Three charts: revenue (line),
  // leads (bars) and channels (donut). In pjs list syntax, single arrays are
  // wrapped in one extra [ ] — so labels = [["Jan","Feb",…]] etc.
  charts
    enabled = true
    heading = Performance at a glance
    description = A live look at the metrics your team cares about most — revenue, pipeline and where your traffic comes from.
    revenue
      title = Monthly revenue
      labels = [["Jan","Feb","Mar","Apr","May","Jun","Jul","Aug","Sep","Oct","Nov","Dec"]]
      series
        s1
          name = This year
          data = [[42,48,45,52,58,63,70,74,82,90,95,112]]
        s2
          name = Last year
          data = [[30,34,38,41,45,49,54,58,61,66,70,78]]
    leads
      title = New leads per month
      labels = [["Jan","Feb","Mar","Apr","May","Jun","Jul","Aug","Sep","Oct","Nov","Dec"]]
      values = [[120,135,128,150,168,182,205,198,226,248,270,305]]
    channels
      title = Traffic sources
      values = [[["Organic search",38],["Paid ads",24],["Referrals",18],["Social media",12],["Direct",8]]]

  // ── "How it works" steps (3) ──────────────────────────────────────────────
  how
    h1
      title = Connect
      description = Bring your existing tools and data. Step-by-step migration guides make the switch painless.
    h2
      title = Configure
      description = Set up roles, branding, and workflows in minutes with guided, zero-code onboarding.
    h3
      title = Launch
      description = Go live with confidence. We monitor, scale, and support you around the clock.

  // ── Showcase section (left column) ────────────────────────────────────────
  showcase
    heading = Built to be dependable
    description = Under the hood, everything is engineered for transparency and control — so your team can move fast without surprises.
    points = [["A single source of truth for your team's workflow","Integrates with the tools you already use","Scales from a small team to the whole enterprise"]]

  // ── Testimonials (3) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  testimonials
    t1
      quote = We rolled this out to our entire team in a day — the smoothest onboarding we've experienced with any platform.
      name = Sarah Chen
      role = Head of Operations
      company = Acme Corp
    t2
      quote = The reliability speaks for itself: zero downtime across six months of heavy production use.
      name = Marcus Reid
      role = CTO
      company = Globex Industries
    t3
      quote = It replaced three separate tools and paid for itself within the first quarter. Our teams love it.
      name = Amara Okafor
      role = Product Lead
      company = Initech

  // ── Final call-to-action band ─────────────────────────────────────────────
  cta
    heading = Ready to take the next step?
    description = Join the teams that ship with confidence. Set up your workspace today — no credit card required.
    buttonText = Get started today
    buttonUrl = #

  // ── Product manual (documentation chapters) ──────────────────────────────
  // A docs-style section with a contents sidebar. Each chapter renders as a
  // numbered card with a title and body text. Add/remove chapters like m1, m2…
  // (body is a single string — use the settings panel or a JSON import if you
  // want real paragraph breaks).
  manual
    heading = Product manual
    description = A guided walkthrough of the product — from first log-in to everyday workflows, admin and support.
    chapters
      m1
        title = Getting started
        body = Create your account, invite teammates, and complete your first project from template to launch in under an hour.
      m2
        title = Installation & setup
        body = Guided onboarding covers roles, integrations and branding, with step-by-step migration guides that make switching from your existing tools painless.
      m3
        title = Everyday workflows
        body = Build, review and ship from one workspace. Dashboards, notifications and automations keep the whole team aligned.
      m4
        title = Admin, billing & support
        body = Manage users and permissions, review usage and invoices, and reach our 24/7 support team from the help center.

  // ── FAQ accordion ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  faq
    q1
      question = How long does it take to get started?
      answer = Most teams are up and running within a day. Guided onboarding covers setup, team invites, and integration with your existing tools.
    q2
      question = Can we use our existing tools and data?
      answer = Yes. Migration guides and adapters cover the most common tools and formats, and our team can assist with custom integrations.
    q3
      question = Is our data secure?
      answer = Data is encrypted in transit and at rest, access is role-based and audited, and deployments run in regions of your choice.
    q4
      question = What does it cost?
      answer = Pricing scales with your team and usage. Contact us for a tailored quote — most teams start small and grow with the platform.
    q5
      question = What kind of support do we get?
      answer = Every plan includes access to documentation and community support. Priority plans add a 24/7 support line with guaranteed response times.

  // ── Footer link columns ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
  footer
    columns
      col1
        heading = Product
        links
          l1
            label = Features
            url = #features
          l2
            label = How it works
            url = #how
          l3
            label = Pricing
            url = #cta
          l4
            label = FAQ
            url = #faq
      col2
        heading = Company
        links
          l1
            label = About
            url = #
          l2
            label = Careers
            url = #
          l3
            label = Contact
            url = #
          l4
            label = Press kit
            url = #
      col3
        heading = Resources
        links
          l1
            label = Documentation
            url = #
          l2
            label = API reference
            url = #
          l3
            label = System status
            url = #
          l4
            label = Community
            url = #

  // ── Editor / admin behavior ───────────────────────────────────────────────
  admin
    showSettingsButton = true         // shows the ⚙ panel used to configure, scan & export

  // ── ERP application settings ──────────────────────────────────────────────
  // Read by src/erp.js at boot. Role-aware module visibility (Task 1) uses
  // roleDefault until the server-authorised role model lands in Phase 10.
  erp
    version = 0.1.0
    roleDefault = owner               // owner | manager | staff
